WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.312 --> 00:00:02.067
- There are all these
business books that say,

00:00:02.067 --> 00:00:04.359
"Hey, be humble, be humble, be humble,"

00:00:04.359 --> 00:00:06.554
but humility is not a natural state

00:00:06.554 --> 00:00:08.124
for a kick-ass human being.

00:00:08.124 --> 00:00:10.977
I was writing terms and
conditions for websites,

00:00:10.977 --> 00:00:12.990
some of which turned out
to be adult websites,

00:00:12.990 --> 00:00:14.209
but they paid cash.

00:00:14.209 --> 00:00:16.924
And Travis Kalanick,
while being CEO of Uber,

00:00:16.924 --> 00:00:19.527
was ranked number two in
the world at Wii Tennis.

00:00:19.527 --> 00:00:21.362
(laughter)

00:00:21.362 --> 00:00:23.203
Who here wants to compete
with a guy like that?

00:00:23.203 --> 00:00:25.771
Some of you aren't
founders, and you should

00:00:25.771 --> 00:00:27.407
admit that to yourselves.

00:00:27.407 --> 00:00:30.374
- He's a former lawyer,
venture capital investor,

00:00:30.374 --> 00:00:32.406
company advisor, and entrepreneur.

00:00:32.406 --> 00:00:34.689
He's the proprietor of Lowercase Capital,

00:00:34.689 --> 00:00:36.932
a venture capital fund
in the United States.

00:00:36.932 --> 00:00:39.336
He invested in seed and
early stage companies like

00:00:39.336 --> 00:00:41.818
Twitter, Uber, Instagram, and Kickstarter.

00:00:41.818 --> 00:00:43.498
He's Chris Sacca, and here's my take

00:00:43.498 --> 00:00:45.442
on his top ten rules of success.

00:00:45.442 --> 00:00:47.024
Rule number five is my personal favorite.

00:00:47.024 --> 00:00:48.595
I'd make sure to stick
around all the way to the end

00:00:48.595 --> 00:00:50.900
for some special bonus
clips, and as always,

00:00:50.900 --> 00:00:52.754
as Chris is talking, if he
says something that really

00:00:52.754 --> 00:00:55.363
resonates with you, please
leave it in the comments below

00:00:55.363 --> 00:00:56.947
and put quotes around it, so other

00:00:56.947 --> 00:00:58.616
people can be inspired as well.

00:00:58.616 --> 00:00:59.449
Enjoy.

00:00:59.449 --> 00:01:02.141
(swoosh)

00:01:02.141 --> 00:01:04.724
(upbeat music)

00:01:12.581 --> 00:01:14.624
- Too many investors
invest in the things that

00:01:14.624 --> 00:01:17.344
they just like and are
comfortable with and are within

00:01:17.344 --> 00:01:19.207
their own little bubbles,

00:01:19.207 --> 00:01:21.611
and yet, I think the
distinction we like to make is

00:01:21.611 --> 00:01:23.293
invest in things we know we can

00:01:23.293 --> 00:01:25.902
personally impact the outcome of, so

00:01:25.902 --> 00:01:28.730
everyone can be talking
about the hot biotech field,

00:01:28.730 --> 00:01:31.227
and it might be on the
verge of curing cancer,

00:01:31.227 --> 00:01:33.652
but I cannot do anything
to advance the mission

00:01:33.652 --> 00:01:36.114
of a biotech company, quite literally,

00:01:36.114 --> 00:01:38.685
and so we pass, as cool as it may seem.

00:01:38.685 --> 00:01:40.905
If I'm going to mooch, I might
as well mooch in the public

00:01:40.905 --> 00:01:42.830
markets, right, and just throw darts

00:01:42.830 --> 00:01:45.292
and ride the greens and
reds, so if I believe

00:01:45.292 --> 00:01:47.727
that I can take something
that's already a good company

00:01:47.727 --> 00:01:51.185
and just make it a little
more likely to be a success,

00:01:51.185 --> 00:01:53.121
then that's a rigged game for me.

00:01:53.121 --> 00:01:54.706
It almost feels unfair, right?

00:01:54.706 --> 00:01:57.242
And I can just be that
much more confident that

00:01:57.242 --> 00:01:59.083
the investors who give me their money,

00:01:59.083 --> 00:02:00.521
I'm going to give them their money back

00:02:00.521 --> 00:02:01.839
and a lot more after that.

00:02:01.839 --> 00:02:04.751
I'm a big believer in the
liberal arts education.

00:02:04.751 --> 00:02:07.178
I think it's overrated, or
it's underrated, I mean.

00:02:07.178 --> 00:02:09.557
In that, the computer
science stuff is overrated.

00:02:09.557 --> 00:02:11.640
It's great, technical skills are amazing,

00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:14.761
but what has happened,
as I referred to earlier

00:02:14.761 --> 00:02:17.406
is we've just, all the storytellers

00:02:17.406 --> 00:02:19.296
are kind of gone, and the actual

00:02:19.296 --> 00:02:21.528
understanding of our users is gone.

00:02:21.528 --> 00:02:23.308
We've lost all that sensitivity,

00:02:23.308 --> 00:02:25.574
and yet I feel like that
sensitivity comes first

00:02:25.574 --> 00:02:27.721
from really knowing yourself, so

00:02:27.721 --> 00:02:29.890
I encourage people to take classes,

00:02:29.890 --> 00:02:33.012
not just in art and music,
but in death and dying

00:02:33.012 --> 00:02:35.669
and philosophy and
theology, these things that

00:02:35.669 --> 00:02:37.920
you'll never make money on directly,

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:39.247
but I think one of the
most important things

00:02:39.247 --> 00:02:41.189
you can do is be interesting.

00:02:41.189 --> 00:02:44.759
I only really want to
work with people who have

00:02:44.759 --> 00:02:46.797
not only had those crappy
jobs I've talked about,

00:02:46.797 --> 00:02:48.096
but are also interesting.

00:02:48.096 --> 00:02:48.929
They have stories.

00:02:48.929 --> 00:02:50.278
There's some meat to them.

00:02:50.278 --> 00:02:51.111
There's some flesh.

00:02:51.111 --> 00:02:52.886
There's something really compelling.

00:02:52.886 --> 00:02:54.189
That's who you want to be with

00:02:54.189 --> 00:02:55.164
if you're going to spend night

00:02:55.164 --> 00:02:56.911
and day building something together.

00:02:56.911 --> 00:02:58.134
That's who you want to travel with,

00:02:58.134 --> 00:03:00.338
these people with these
perspectives and these stories,

00:03:00.338 --> 00:03:02.569
and so, and interestingness
is not something

00:03:02.569 --> 00:03:03.605
that I think you're born with.

00:03:03.605 --> 00:03:05.886
I think it's achieved by

00:03:05.886 --> 00:03:08.043
vulnerability, by adventure,
by all these things,

00:03:08.043 --> 00:03:10.481
and so I really emphasize interestingness.

00:03:10.481 --> 00:03:12.392
I think you can get it from

00:03:12.392 --> 00:03:14.357
from studying, from reading, from putting

00:03:14.357 --> 00:03:16.437
yourself in uncomfortable positions.

00:03:16.437 --> 00:03:19.306
Maybe more Americans should
just hang out naked in saunas.

00:03:19.306 --> 00:03:21.072
That could make them more interesting,

00:03:21.072 --> 00:03:24.193
but traveling abroad, going to

00:03:24.193 --> 00:03:26.776
Ethiopia and helping dig wells.

00:03:27.718 --> 00:03:30.458
That kind of stuff I
think builds a much more

00:03:30.458 --> 00:03:33.600
impressive character,
so Stewart Butterfield,

00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:35.634
who runs Slack, obviously one of the most

00:03:35.634 --> 00:03:38.991
successful investments in our
portfolio, an amazing company.

00:03:38.991 --> 00:03:41.429
Stewart is a liberal arts major.

00:03:41.429 --> 00:03:43.526
That do is fascinating.

00:03:43.526 --> 00:03:45.475
Like hanging out with that guy and talking

00:03:45.475 --> 00:03:47.717
about anything beyond Slack is amazing.

00:03:47.717 --> 00:03:49.029
He's Canadian, and yet he's got

00:03:49.029 --> 00:03:51.126
great insights into U.S. politics.

00:03:51.126 --> 00:03:53.780
But you just follow his snaps on Snapchat.

00:03:53.780 --> 00:03:57.061
The guy is, he eats well,
he's checking out art,

00:03:57.061 --> 00:04:00.292
he knows music really well,
he's a really interesting guy,

00:04:00.292 --> 00:04:02.265
so he becomes a magnet for talent.

00:04:02.265 --> 00:04:03.863
You want to be around Stewart.

00:04:03.863 --> 00:04:06.472
He enriches your soul, and
if you look at the team

00:04:06.472 --> 00:04:08.622
of people he's recruited, super stars

00:04:08.622 --> 00:04:09.797
like April Underwood, for instance.

00:04:09.797 --> 00:04:13.176
He's really recruited an
incredibly deep bench of people

00:04:13.176 --> 00:04:16.110
because they want to have
authentic and multi-dimensional

00:04:16.110 --> 00:04:17.707
relationships with him, and I think

00:04:17.707 --> 00:04:19.431
it comes through in the product too.

00:04:19.431 --> 00:04:20.661
It's time for step three.

00:04:20.661 --> 00:04:22.676
Step three is to go on offense.

00:04:22.676 --> 00:04:24.394
Now what do I mean by going on offense?

00:04:24.394 --> 00:04:25.841
Well, you guys all know sports.

00:04:25.841 --> 00:04:26.793
This is the Big 10.

00:04:26.793 --> 00:04:28.939
You can't avoid sports here.

00:04:28.939 --> 00:04:30.448
You know what it means to be on offense.

00:04:30.448 --> 00:04:33.449
Offense is calling plays,
managing the clock,

00:04:33.449 --> 00:04:37.117
keeping the other team on
defense reacting to you.

00:04:37.117 --> 00:04:40.628
Now take that term and apply
it to the rest of your life.

00:04:40.628 --> 00:04:43.348
Let's take for example, e-mail.

00:04:43.348 --> 00:04:45.964
Is your e-mail inbox offense?

00:04:45.964 --> 00:04:47.395
Is that playing offense?

00:04:47.395 --> 00:04:51.779
No, no, no, inbox stuff is
just defense the whole way.

00:04:51.779 --> 00:04:54.275
Imagine if you took your to do list,

00:04:54.275 --> 00:04:57.224
and you posted it on the
door of your dorm room

00:04:57.224 --> 00:04:59.841
with a little pencil there
and invited the entire world

00:04:59.841 --> 00:05:03.221
to come and add anything they
wanted to your to do list.

00:05:03.221 --> 00:05:04.964
Well that's what your inbox is.

00:05:04.964 --> 00:05:06.539
It's a publicly posted to do list

00:05:06.539 --> 00:05:10.353
where it's up to everybody else
to impose their whim on you.

00:05:10.353 --> 00:05:13.008
And even worse, what happens
is we get up and it's the very

00:05:13.008 --> 00:05:14.933
first thing we check,
and so we get in there,

00:05:14.933 --> 00:05:16.876
and we start doing stuff
for all these other people,

00:05:16.876 --> 00:05:18.519
and yet, along the way,
we've forgotten what

00:05:18.519 --> 00:05:21.321
we wanted to accomplish for ourselves.

00:05:21.321 --> 00:05:22.664
We haven't done the things that we know

00:05:22.664 --> 00:05:24.605
are important for us and for our goals.

00:05:24.605 --> 00:05:26.663
And so that stops now.

00:05:26.663 --> 00:05:28.882
It's time to close your e-mail

00:05:28.882 --> 00:05:30.746
and write out your to do list.

00:05:30.746 --> 00:05:32.076
I don't just mean your
to do list for work.

00:05:32.076 --> 00:05:34.235
I mean the big to do list for your life.

00:05:34.235 --> 00:05:36.771
What do you want to get done?

00:05:36.771 --> 00:05:38.038
It's an awesome time in history

00:05:38.038 --> 00:05:40.136
to be authoring our own
futures in real time.

00:05:40.136 --> 00:05:42.654
I mean the internet makes it
possible to be just one degree

00:05:42.654 --> 00:05:44.315
away from anyone in the world.

00:05:44.315 --> 00:05:46.973
Any one of you right now can
send me a reply on Twitter

00:05:46.973 --> 00:05:50.898
if you had your phones out,
and I'm that accessible.

00:05:50.898 --> 00:05:53.567
Any one of you right now could
use the internet to become

00:05:53.567 --> 00:05:55.447
an expert in any subject at all.

00:05:55.447 --> 00:05:57.331
In 2007 back when I was at Google,

00:05:57.331 --> 00:05:59.870
I went from not knowing
anything whatsoever

00:05:59.870 --> 00:06:01.783
about the wireless industry

00:06:01.783 --> 00:06:04.719
to leading a group that
led a 4.7 billion dollar

00:06:04.719 --> 00:06:08.074
project that turned Verizon,
AT&amp;T on their heads,

00:06:08.074 --> 00:06:10.671
sent the FCC spinning,
and changed the landscape

00:06:10.671 --> 00:06:13.255
of wireless forever,
paving the way for the apps

00:06:13.255 --> 00:06:15.634
we all have on our phones today.

00:06:15.634 --> 00:06:19.644
Never in history have their
been fewer barriers in the way.

00:06:19.644 --> 00:06:21.673
No matter where we come
from, what our parents did

00:06:21.673 --> 00:06:23.377
for a living, how much money we have,

00:06:23.377 --> 00:06:26.377
we all can be unqualified successes.

00:06:27.489 --> 00:06:29.122
So who do you want to talk to?

00:06:29.122 --> 00:06:30.439
Where do you want to spend your time?

00:06:30.439 --> 00:06:32.146
What do you want to learn?

00:06:32.146 --> 00:06:34.619
You just spent four
years, maybe five or six,

00:06:34.619 --> 00:06:37.485
but we'll just forget that,
four years for the most part,

00:06:37.485 --> 00:06:40.972
taking the classes you
were required to take,

00:06:40.972 --> 00:06:43.445
so now it's your turn to go on offense.

00:06:43.445 --> 00:06:45.190
What are you curious about?

00:06:45.190 --> 00:06:46.140
Where do you want to travel?

00:06:46.140 --> 00:06:47.506
What adventures do you want to go on?

00:06:47.506 --> 00:06:50.733
Which fears of yours
do you want to tackle?

00:06:50.733 --> 00:06:54.186
You know offense also means
owning your personal brand.

00:06:54.186 --> 00:06:58.353
Your voice should be the
first two or three responses

00:06:59.193 --> 00:07:01.462
when I search for your name on Twitter.

00:07:01.462 --> 00:07:02.694
I'm sorry on Google.

00:07:02.694 --> 00:07:05.313
But how do I, how do you get there?

00:07:05.313 --> 00:07:07.718
You Tweet, you post,
you blog, you comment,

00:07:07.718 --> 00:07:11.643
you engage in discussions,
you own your own identity.

00:07:11.643 --> 00:07:13.750
The tools are there to
have a public identity

00:07:13.750 --> 00:07:15.642
and to go on the record, proudly so,

00:07:15.642 --> 00:07:18.153
and stand for who you really are.

00:07:18.153 --> 00:07:19.208
I mean think of it.

00:07:19.208 --> 00:07:21.211
I hire a lot of people for a lot of jobs.

00:07:21.211 --> 00:07:24.151
Imagine, why would I ever hire
somebody just on the basis

00:07:24.151 --> 00:07:28.196
of a resume, when instead I
can search for them online

00:07:28.196 --> 00:07:30.600
and get years and years worth
of insight into who they

00:07:30.600 --> 00:07:33.155
really are and how they think
and how their ideas evolve

00:07:33.155 --> 00:07:36.362
and how they react to the
changing world we're in.

00:07:36.362 --> 00:07:38.752
Those tools exist for
you to create your own

00:07:38.752 --> 00:07:42.151
authentic brand, completely
within your control.

00:07:42.151 --> 00:07:42.984
And do it.

00:07:44.213 --> 00:07:46.002
I mean sitting back and
watching television,

00:07:46.002 --> 00:07:49.002
that's playing defense,
but going outside and being

00:07:49.002 --> 00:07:51.733
with your friends, that's offense.

00:07:51.733 --> 00:07:54.916
Provoke, inspire, don't just get involved,

00:07:54.916 --> 00:07:56.341
but inspire others to be involved.

00:07:56.341 --> 00:07:57.901
That's playing offense.

00:07:57.901 --> 00:08:00.607
In the spring of 2000, I was way

00:08:00.607 --> 00:08:02.290
over-leveraged into these two stocks.

00:08:02.290 --> 00:08:03.924
In a matter of a few
days, I found myself going

00:08:03.924 --> 00:08:05.949
from 12 million dollars to
the good of four million

00:08:05.949 --> 00:08:08.518
dollars to the bad, so I was 25 years old,

00:08:08.518 --> 00:08:09.664
I owed 4 million dollars.

00:08:09.664 --> 00:08:10.820
I didn't have anything to show for it.

00:08:10.820 --> 00:08:12.712
I didn't have a house or a
boat or a plane or anything

00:08:12.712 --> 00:08:14.525
I could sell to pay it off.

00:08:14.525 --> 00:08:17.031
I just owed, I owed it, and I came

00:08:17.031 --> 00:08:18.379
from a middle class background.

00:08:18.379 --> 00:08:20.220
Neither of my parents ever made more

00:08:20.220 --> 00:08:23.571
than a hundred grand in a
year, and I was just stuck.

00:08:23.571 --> 00:08:25.194
And I had to make some choices then

00:08:25.194 --> 00:08:28.514
about whether I was going to quit, and

00:08:28.514 --> 00:08:29.809
or you know or try to declare bankruptcy

00:08:29.809 --> 00:08:31.703
or whether I'd try and fight it out.

00:08:31.703 --> 00:08:32.953
And it happened

00:08:35.161 --> 00:08:38.362
just as I was graduating
law school actually,

00:08:38.362 --> 00:08:40.775
and so I said, "Well I'm going
to try and fight this out,

00:08:40.775 --> 00:08:43.625
"and I'll take a sellout
job as a corporate lawyer

00:08:43.625 --> 00:08:45.749
"out at Fenwick and West,

00:08:45.749 --> 00:08:47.006
"and I'll try and grind this out."

00:08:47.006 --> 00:08:49.257
And so during the day I
would do corporate law stuff

00:08:49.257 --> 00:08:52.598
at a tech firm, and at
night, I would do pretty much

00:08:52.598 --> 00:08:54.721
anything that somebody would pay me to do,

00:08:54.721 --> 00:08:57.208
short of giving blood or massages.

00:08:57.208 --> 00:09:00.001
- Did you use like Craigslist for this?

00:09:00.001 --> 00:09:01.147
- I did go on Craigslist.

00:09:01.147 --> 00:09:04.631
I was writing contracts and business plans

00:09:04.631 --> 00:09:06.229
and renaming companies.

00:09:06.229 --> 00:09:07.542
There's a thing called Elance out there.

00:09:07.542 --> 00:09:09.905
There's now like oDesk and
Rent a coder and stuff,

00:09:09.905 --> 00:09:12.213
but Elance was the first of those markets,

00:09:12.213 --> 00:09:13.906
and I was the first lawyer on Elance,

00:09:13.906 --> 00:09:17.235
and so I was writing terms and conditions

00:09:17.235 --> 00:09:19.576
for websites, some of which turned out

00:09:19.576 --> 00:09:21.951
to be adult websites, but they paid cash.

00:09:21.951 --> 00:09:23.463
(laughter)

00:09:23.463 --> 00:09:27.296
And so I even did a
voiceover for a book once.

00:09:29.534 --> 00:09:32.801
Yeah, who knew, but I actually
screamed for a few hours

00:09:32.801 --> 00:09:33.971
to make my voice raspy, that
was what they were looking

00:09:33.971 --> 00:09:35.702
for in the character, so,

00:09:35.702 --> 00:09:38.130
but I just did anything I
could to try and pay off

00:09:38.130 --> 00:09:41.463
this debt, and it took me from then 'til

00:09:42.754 --> 00:09:45.065
basically the Google IPO,
to selling another company

00:09:45.065 --> 00:09:48.869
in the Google IPO before I
got back to zero of networth.

00:09:48.869 --> 00:09:51.147
And I'll tell you, no matter how early

00:09:51.147 --> 00:09:53.682
you are in your career, the
richest you will ever feel

00:09:53.682 --> 00:09:56.841
is when you owe zero dollars to anybody.

00:09:56.841 --> 00:09:58.304
That's the very first time in your life

00:09:58.304 --> 00:10:00.168
when you've got choices.

00:10:00.168 --> 00:10:02.312
You can, you could go
join the Peace Corps,

00:10:02.312 --> 00:10:03.362
you could go travel the world,

00:10:03.362 --> 00:10:04.225
you could do anything.

00:10:04.225 --> 00:10:07.894
I actually celebrated by throwing
a BYOB party at my house.

00:10:07.894 --> 00:10:10.722
Like I'm not going into the
red to treat all you guys, so.

00:10:10.722 --> 00:10:12.247
(laughter)

00:10:12.247 --> 00:10:16.275
But that was a real
pivotal moment in my life.

00:10:16.275 --> 00:10:18.025
I'll tell you, I just

00:10:19.678 --> 00:10:21.658
I never really considered
any other option.

00:10:21.658 --> 00:10:24.473
I never considered moving
back with my parents

00:10:24.473 --> 00:10:25.524
to Buffalo, New York.

00:10:25.524 --> 00:10:27.402
I never considered declaring bankruptcy.

00:10:27.402 --> 00:10:30.728
I just kind of always knew I
would fight my way out of it,

00:10:30.728 --> 00:10:32.943
and I think that's something that,

00:10:32.943 --> 00:10:35.776
for those of you who are founders,

00:10:37.012 --> 00:10:39.271
I'd encourage, not everybody's a founder.

00:10:39.271 --> 00:10:40.824
It's easy to kind of sign the paperwork,

00:10:40.824 --> 00:10:42.932
but some of you just aren't founders,

00:10:42.932 --> 00:10:45.760
and there's a time when you
have to admit that to yourself

00:10:45.760 --> 00:10:47.442
that it's cool to have a startup,

00:10:47.442 --> 00:10:49.859
but you may not be a founder.

00:10:50.845 --> 00:10:54.641
You may not be the kind of unstable,

00:10:54.641 --> 00:10:56.861
mentally askew person

00:10:56.861 --> 00:11:00.611
with a slightly
manic-depressive side, who is

00:11:01.469 --> 00:11:04.165
who knows in the face
of insurmountable odds

00:11:04.165 --> 00:11:06.029
that you're going to win, and I don't mean

00:11:06.029 --> 00:11:08.394
is convinced that you can sell people

00:11:08.394 --> 00:11:09.710
and convince people of that.

00:11:09.710 --> 00:11:12.162
I mean you know in your bones

00:11:12.162 --> 00:11:13.942
that you're going to make it out,

00:11:13.942 --> 00:11:16.063
and I think that's something
that was a little different

00:11:16.063 --> 00:11:18.414
about me, was like, in that
whole as advisor was just

00:11:18.414 --> 00:11:20.301
saying, "Look just go bk
and seven years from now,

00:11:20.301 --> 00:11:21.837
"you can walk away from it."

00:11:21.837 --> 00:11:24.873
I just knew I would
fight my way out of it.

00:11:24.873 --> 00:11:27.985
And I didn't have to convince
myself or anybody else.

00:11:27.985 --> 00:11:31.707
- I mean, you are
unforgivingly who you are.

00:11:31.707 --> 00:11:32.819
Have you always been like that?

00:11:32.819 --> 00:11:33.794
- [Chris] No.

00:11:33.794 --> 00:11:34.928
- [Interviewer] What happened?

00:11:34.928 --> 00:11:35.862
- Like when I was in college--

00:11:35.862 --> 00:11:37.864
- [Interviewer] What were you
like, were you ever insecure?

00:11:37.864 --> 00:11:40.556
- I still am insecure, like
there's probably people

00:11:40.556 --> 00:11:42.422
snickering backstage right
now at that question like

00:11:42.422 --> 00:11:44.989
(snickers)

00:11:44.989 --> 00:11:46.823
I, when I was in college,
I didn't want to come out

00:11:46.823 --> 00:11:48.996
as a progressive because I
would eliminate like half

00:11:48.996 --> 00:11:52.092
of my future employers,
and in Washington, D.C.,

00:11:52.092 --> 00:11:54.016
I was like, look I got
to look neutral here,

00:11:54.016 --> 00:11:56.266
so I can get a job someday.

00:11:58.067 --> 00:11:59.389
I found

00:11:59.389 --> 00:12:00.939
progressively, and you know,

00:12:00.939 --> 00:12:02.231
some of these lessons
were foisted upon me.

00:12:02.231 --> 00:12:03.064
I mean, I mentioned earlier,

00:12:03.064 --> 00:12:04.862
so not to repeat a story, but.

00:12:04.862 --> 00:12:07.446
It's at Google, I'm working on this

00:12:07.446 --> 00:12:09.453
team and this wireless stuff.

00:12:09.453 --> 00:12:11.295
It was an incredible
amount of responsibility.

00:12:11.295 --> 00:12:13.710
Larry had given me, Larry
Page had given me billions

00:12:13.710 --> 00:12:16.234
of dollars to go take on
the wireless carriers,

00:12:16.234 --> 00:12:18.393
and I was in over my head.

00:12:18.393 --> 00:12:19.815
I didn't have a background in this stuff,

00:12:19.815 --> 00:12:22.402
and I was learning fast,
and I was pretty good at it,

00:12:22.402 --> 00:12:24.438
but one day a decision came
up, and I didn't know what

00:12:24.438 --> 00:12:27.815
to do, and my work partner
wrote in the notes,

00:12:27.815 --> 00:12:29.619
Chris doesn't know what to do about this,

00:12:29.619 --> 00:12:32.315
and at Google, the notes are
published to the whole company,

00:12:32.315 --> 00:12:34.326
so I see the notes get published,

00:12:34.326 --> 00:12:35.251
and I'm like, "What'd you just do?

00:12:35.251 --> 00:12:36.152
"You just ruined my career.

00:12:36.152 --> 00:12:39.727
"Everyone's going to know
I'm smoke and mirrors."

00:12:39.727 --> 00:12:42.711
And I thought it was over for me,

00:12:42.711 --> 00:12:45.124
and then the next meeting,
some new engineers,

00:12:45.124 --> 00:12:47.952
who didn't report to me, and
Larry Page, himself showed up

00:12:47.952 --> 00:12:49.643
with ideas on what to do
to solve this problem,

00:12:49.643 --> 00:12:52.615
and I was like, "Oh my god,
I just became more powerful.

00:12:52.615 --> 00:12:53.448
"I now have influence.

00:12:53.448 --> 00:12:54.798
"I have more bodies working for me,

00:12:54.798 --> 00:12:56.490
"who don't even report to me."

00:12:56.490 --> 00:12:58.501
And it was, I mean I wish I could say

00:12:58.501 --> 00:13:00.611
it was an enlightened choice I'd made.

00:13:00.611 --> 00:13:02.732
It was totally foist upon
me, and I was angry about it,

00:13:02.732 --> 00:13:06.899
but it worked, and so along
the way, I realized that

00:13:08.017 --> 00:13:11.351
the candor, the authenticity,
the vulnerability,

00:13:11.351 --> 00:13:14.432
is what actually attracts
the other people to you.

00:13:14.432 --> 00:13:17.091
It's, the insecurity
roots you in this place

00:13:17.091 --> 00:13:19.447
of showing you're impervious,

00:13:19.447 --> 00:13:21.112
that you've got all the answers,

00:13:21.112 --> 00:13:23.749
but what that does is it
renders everyone around you

00:13:23.749 --> 00:13:26.829
helpless, they're just like,
"I'm not valued here," right?

00:13:26.829 --> 00:13:29.572
And so when you start to admit
I don't know what I'm doing.

00:13:29.572 --> 00:13:30.980
I need some help.

00:13:30.980 --> 00:13:33.296
Then people start attracting
to you and working with you.

00:13:33.296 --> 00:13:36.052
I mean, I have a principle
that I guide my life by,

00:13:36.052 --> 00:13:37.323
and I try to teach this to our

00:13:37.323 --> 00:13:39.102
entrepreneurs, called bold humility.

00:13:39.102 --> 00:13:41.419
There are all these
business books that say,

00:13:41.419 --> 00:13:42.901
"Hey, be humble, be humble, be humble,"

00:13:42.901 --> 00:13:44.841
but humility is not a natural state

00:13:44.841 --> 00:13:47.534
for a kick-ass human being, right?

00:13:47.534 --> 00:13:51.595
If you're here, you probably
kick a lot of ass at something.

00:13:51.595 --> 00:13:53.692
You're probably the best at something.

00:13:53.692 --> 00:13:56.031
You have ambition, you're a risk taker,

00:13:56.031 --> 00:13:59.334
you have learned to hack,
you are a good talker,

00:13:59.334 --> 00:14:01.396
you have through the gift of gab

00:14:01.396 --> 00:14:03.224
wound your way into an amazing situation,

00:14:03.224 --> 00:14:05.928
you hustle, hustle, hustle,
and I feel like it's

00:14:05.928 --> 00:14:08.891
unnatural to apologize
for that, but where we get

00:14:08.891 --> 00:14:11.562
into trouble is when we feel like we have

00:14:11.562 --> 00:14:14.303
to extend that to the shit we don't know,

00:14:14.303 --> 00:14:16.341
