laundry bin here in the men's locker room,

00:20:16.636 --> 00:20:18.045
but that still doesn't
make you a great person,

00:20:18.045 --> 00:20:20.873
and it definitely doesn't
mean anyone will like you.

00:20:20.873 --> 00:20:22.604
I mean, don't get me wrong,
as I mentioned before,

00:20:22.604 --> 00:20:23.994
you're never going to feel richer

00:20:23.994 --> 00:20:25.659
than when you've paid
off those credit cards

00:20:25.659 --> 00:20:27.675
and student loans, but after that,

00:20:27.675 --> 00:20:30.051
money tends to be pretty unfulfilling.

00:20:30.051 --> 00:20:33.234
Almost all the people I know,
who've made a lot of money,

00:20:33.234 --> 00:20:36.049
they've turned into that guy.

00:20:36.049 --> 00:20:39.060
All right, well you know
the guy who pops his collar,

00:20:39.060 --> 00:20:40.877
and if he, and if it were up
to him, and he were wearing

00:20:40.877 --> 00:20:42.799
two shirts, he could pop both collars.

00:20:42.799 --> 00:20:43.896
(laughter)

00:20:43.896 --> 00:20:45.146
That's the guy.

00:20:47.271 --> 00:20:48.989
So how will we know when
we've done it right?

00:20:48.989 --> 00:20:50.831
What's the ultimate
measure of success then?

00:20:50.831 --> 00:20:52.318
What's the key to success?

00:20:52.318 --> 00:20:55.122
Well in my experience, quite simply,

00:20:55.122 --> 00:20:56.872
success is happiness.

00:20:58.340 --> 00:21:00.206
It all comes down to happiness,

00:21:00.206 --> 00:21:02.426
and I definitely don't mean
that happiness that you feel

00:21:02.426 --> 00:21:04.303
when the Gophers take over on downs

00:21:04.303 --> 00:21:07.117
in their own red zone,
as great as that feels.

00:21:07.117 --> 00:21:11.145
I mean that happiness that quite
literally fills your lungs.

00:21:11.145 --> 00:21:12.422
The kind of happiness that makes a noise.

00:21:12.422 --> 00:21:14.234
It announces its arrival with

00:21:14.234 --> 00:21:16.575
a deep inhale and a deep exhale.

00:21:16.575 --> 00:21:18.737
(loud breathing)

00:21:18.737 --> 00:21:20.087
That happiness.

00:21:20.087 --> 00:21:21.600
You know that happiness, right?

00:21:21.600 --> 00:21:23.721
That happiness is success because

00:21:23.721 --> 00:21:27.465
it turns out happy people get jobs.

00:21:27.465 --> 00:21:29.012
Happy people create.

00:21:29.012 --> 00:21:30.621
Happy people win.

00:21:30.621 --> 00:21:33.000
Happy people fall in
love and raise families.

00:21:33.000 --> 00:21:35.194
They lead and change the world.

00:21:35.194 --> 00:21:36.901
Happy people get what they want

00:21:36.901 --> 00:21:39.203
because often happy people
are the ones who realize

00:21:39.203 --> 00:21:41.972
they don't actually need much
more than they already have,

00:21:41.972 --> 00:21:44.090
and they appreciate what they've got.

00:21:44.090 --> 00:21:45.848
Happy people are contagious.

00:21:45.848 --> 00:21:47.663
Some of you aren't founders and you

00:21:47.663 --> 00:21:49.575
should admit that to yourselves.

00:21:49.575 --> 00:21:53.075
So it's become very
fashionable to start something.

00:21:53.075 --> 00:21:56.367
It's amazing you know when
Justin Timberlake plays somebody

00:21:56.367 --> 00:21:59.672
in a move about, you know,
about a startup that's awesome.

00:21:59.672 --> 00:22:01.501
There are lots of people
making a ton of money

00:22:01.501 --> 00:22:05.643
and on magazine covers,
but this is a very special,

00:22:05.643 --> 00:22:10.034
different journey that is not
actually suited to everybody.

00:22:10.034 --> 00:22:12.326
You have to be a little weird,

00:22:12.326 --> 00:22:14.350
a little crazy, a little obsessive.

00:22:14.350 --> 00:22:16.566
You probably have some personal issues

00:22:16.566 --> 00:22:18.529
that make you different, like you

00:22:18.529 --> 00:22:20.370
have some demons a little bit.

00:22:20.370 --> 00:22:22.956
You may have trouble relating
to other human beings

00:22:22.956 --> 00:22:25.977
from time to time, like you
don't accept failure very well.

00:22:25.977 --> 00:22:28.013
Maybe you go off a little
bit, maybe you struggle

00:22:28.013 --> 00:22:30.417
a little bit with manic
depression, et cetera.

00:22:30.417 --> 00:22:32.816
If none of those things
sound familiar to you,

00:22:32.816 --> 00:22:34.561
you should probably quit right now.

00:22:34.561 --> 00:22:36.813
(laughter)

00:22:36.813 --> 00:22:38.804
And so sorry if that's a
little bit of a downer.

00:22:38.804 --> 00:22:41.616
I just, some of you should be employees.

00:22:41.616 --> 00:22:42.827
Some of you should be partners.

00:22:42.827 --> 00:22:44.381
Some of you should be employee number,

00:22:44.381 --> 00:22:47.214
you know number one at a
startup, and I think right now.

00:22:47.214 --> 00:22:48.737
Oh that's amazing, they actually

00:22:48.737 --> 00:22:50.262
showed us a hurry up please sign.

00:22:50.262 --> 00:22:51.774
- [Interviewer] Yeah, I know, I divested,

00:22:51.774 --> 00:22:53.919
but that's good, but finish with that

00:22:53.919 --> 00:22:56.284
because we, that's powerful,
and people should hear that.

00:22:56.284 --> 00:22:59.259
- No I, there are founders, and
then there are team members,

00:22:59.259 --> 00:23:02.198
and in an era where it's
been so easy to get capital,

00:23:02.198 --> 00:23:04.598
I just think the posers should step aside.

00:23:04.598 --> 00:23:07.294
I guess I just think our
industry is blind to the impact.

00:23:07.294 --> 00:23:08.127
To how,

00:23:11.299 --> 00:23:13.243
I think we have written

00:23:13.243 --> 00:23:14.326
human beings,

00:23:15.862 --> 00:23:18.668
our emotion, our purpose,

00:23:18.668 --> 00:23:22.090
our community, our collective humanity,

00:23:22.090 --> 00:23:25.913
and our empathy have all been
written out of the algorithm,

00:23:25.913 --> 00:23:29.852
and I think that's going
to cost us in the long run.

00:23:29.852 --> 00:23:33.088
It will not only help the
rise of despotic leaders,

00:23:33.088 --> 00:23:34.769
but I think it's what's actually

00:23:34.769 --> 00:23:38.424
severing our connections
between each other.

00:23:38.424 --> 00:23:39.757
You know I think

00:23:41.130 --> 00:23:42.729
technology has evolved a lot faster

00:23:42.729 --> 00:23:45.509
than our primal brains have.

00:23:45.509 --> 00:23:46.871
And I think

00:23:46.871 --> 00:23:49.775
we've lost track of how
important presence is.

00:23:49.775 --> 00:23:52.222
You know, constant
notifications and buzzing

00:23:52.222 --> 00:23:54.844
and the impact that's
actually having on our health

00:23:54.844 --> 00:23:57.856
and our lives, and so, I
think if we don't wake up

00:23:57.856 --> 00:24:00.523
very soon to the absolute dearth

00:24:01.499 --> 00:24:05.468
of humanity within what we're
building and have that baked

00:24:05.468 --> 00:24:07.270
right into our mission and our values,

00:24:07.270 --> 00:24:10.539
our purpose, our hiring, who we brought

00:24:10.539 --> 00:24:13.372
into these companies,
then we're going to lose.

00:24:13.372 --> 00:24:16.290
We're going to lose not
just on the investor side,

00:24:16.290 --> 00:24:18.156
not just on the product side,

00:24:18.156 --> 00:24:20.510
but the monoculture will eat itself,

00:24:20.510 --> 00:24:24.343
and our entire planet
will suffer as a result.

00:24:25.669 --> 00:24:27.056
- Thank you guys, so much for watching.

00:24:27.056 --> 00:24:29.545
I made this video because
Rajit Lopa asked me to,

00:24:29.545 --> 00:24:32.535
so if there's a famous
entrepreneur that you want

00:24:32.535 --> 00:24:34.895
me to profile next, please
leave it in the comments below,

00:24:34.895 --> 00:24:36.065
and I'll see what I can do.

00:24:36.065 --> 00:24:37.892
I 'd also like to know, what
did you learn from Chris

00:24:37.892 --> 00:24:39.369
that had the biggest impact on you.

00:24:39.369 --> 00:24:41.150
What lesson are you going to apply most

00:24:41.150 --> 00:24:43.125
to your businesses or your life?

00:24:43.125 --> 00:24:44.645
Please leave it in the comments below,

00:24:44.645 --> 00:24:46.479
and I'm going to join in the discussion.

00:24:46.479 --> 00:24:48.254
Finally wanted to give a
quick shout-out to Edwin,

00:24:48.254 --> 00:24:50.971
who is one of the assistance
at Trinidad Salsa.

00:24:50.971 --> 00:24:52.249
Edwin thank you so much for picking up

00:24:52.249 --> 00:24:54.262
a copy of my book, "You One Word."

00:24:54.262 --> 00:24:55.986
It really, really,
really means a lot to me,

00:24:55.986 --> 00:24:58.193
and taking that picture
and posting on Facebook.

00:24:58.193 --> 00:24:59.948
I'm really feeling the love, man.

00:24:59.948 --> 00:25:01.596
So thank you guys again for watching.

00:25:01.596 --> 00:25:02.717
I believe in you.

00:25:02.717 --> 00:25:04.935
I hope you continue to believe in yourself

00:25:04.935 --> 00:25:07.106
and whatever your one word is.

00:25:07.106 --> 00:25:09.820
Much love. I'll see you soon.

00:25:09.820 --> 00:25:12.737
(percussive music)

00:25:15.588 --> 00:25:19.220
- What advice would you give
for someone in their early 20s?

00:25:19.220 --> 00:25:21.376
Living in this era, in
this time of the world.

00:25:21.376 --> 00:25:23.086
- Everyone spend less money.

00:25:23.086 --> 00:25:26.743
I think the freest you'll ever
feel is when you don't owe

00:25:26.743 --> 00:25:28.206
anybody any money, when you're,

00:25:28.206 --> 00:25:30.239
you have all these choices in the world.

00:25:30.239 --> 00:25:32.556
You know, you guys have the benefit of.

00:25:32.556 --> 00:25:33.908
I've never went bankrupt.

00:25:33.908 --> 00:25:35.544
I've lost tons and tons of money.

00:25:35.544 --> 00:25:37.649
Well first of all, in the
United States, we have to pay

00:25:37.649 --> 00:25:39.571
a lot of money for school, so that's one

00:25:39.571 --> 00:25:40.801
of your competitive advantages.

00:25:40.801 --> 00:25:44.273
I left college and then into law school,

00:25:44.273 --> 00:25:46.662
so seven years of school, and I owed

00:25:46.662 --> 00:25:49.320
like 200 thousand dollars, and that was

00:25:49.320 --> 00:25:51.987
twenty years ago, so that's bad.

00:25:53.050 --> 00:25:55.307
I think it's already a competitive
advantage you guys have.

00:25:55.307 --> 00:25:58.815
And you like get to go to
hospitals and yeah, it's amazing.

00:25:58.815 --> 00:26:00.500
All this stuff we're about to break.

00:26:00.500 --> 00:26:01.499
- Daycare.

00:26:01.499 --> 00:26:02.997
- Did I just start talking
about Donald Trump again?

00:26:02.997 --> 00:26:03.830
- Yeah, sorry.

00:26:03.830 --> 00:26:04.663
(laughter)

00:26:04.663 --> 00:26:07.353
- But so, so I just think one
of the most important things

00:26:07.353 --> 00:26:11.946
a young person can do is just
buy less, have less stuff.

00:26:11.946 --> 00:26:16.429
Stuff, just, you're a prisoner
to your things, right?

00:26:16.429 --> 00:26:18.353
And so you can pick up
and travel more easily.

00:26:18.353 --> 00:26:20.521
You can have a smaller house.

00:26:20.521 --> 00:26:24.236
You can quit your job
and go work in an NGO,

00:26:24.236 --> 00:26:27.043
volunteer work, you can pursue
your art career if you have

00:26:27.043 --> 00:26:29.530
less and owe less money to fewer people,

00:26:29.530 --> 00:26:32.363
so I think that's like number one.

00:26:33.370 --> 00:26:36.037
See now you're not even focused.

00:26:37.064 --> 00:26:37.897
- Oh?

00:26:37.897 --> 00:26:40.643
- Now you're just trying too hard.

00:26:40.643 --> 00:26:42.778
- I'm beginning to think
that maybe you and Matt,

00:26:42.778 --> 00:26:45.058
the CEO of Automatic have been playing

00:26:45.058 --> 00:26:47.718
on that table a little
bit, and I got suckered.

00:26:47.718 --> 00:26:50.089
- I told you earlier, I
like to play rigged games.

00:26:50.089 --> 00:26:51.424
I don't invest in companies
where I don't think

00:26:51.424 --> 00:26:53.305
we have a competitive advantage
